The objective of image captioning involves generating human-readable descriptive text from visual input. Recent investigations have demonstrated substantial progress, which is primarily manifested through more sophisticated cross-modal fusion architectures~\cite{you2016image,vinyals2015show, xu2015show,rennie2017self,yang2019auto,cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet}; more expressive object-centric features~\cite{anderson2018bottom,zhang2021multi} \& tags~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} derived from pre-trained object detection models; or acquiring general 	extbf{	extit{V}}ision and 	extbf{	extit{L}}anguage (VL) representations from extensive image-text corpora~\cite{zhou2020unified,xu2021e2e,li2020oscar,wang2021simvlm,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing}. Despite these substantial developments, the majority of prevalent captioning models~\cite{cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet} depend extensively on cumbersome object detectors to furnish regional visual representations for multimodal interaction, as illustrated in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-a}. Notwithstanding the enhanced performance provided by object features, consequent complications arise as they: 1) 	extbf{impose substantial computational burden} owing to regional operations (\ie, RPN, RoI Pooling, and NMS). These intermediate procedures inevitably result in training inefficiency and elevated inference latency during prediction~\cite{kim2021vilt,wang2020minivlm}; 2) 	extbf{necessitate box annotations} and considerably constrain flexibility in training and deployment. To mitigate these challenges, an emerging paradigm has witnessed recent works proposing to eliminate the detector for VL pre-training in an end-to-end manner~\cite{jiang2020defense,huang2020pixel,kim2021vilt,yan2021grid,wang2021simvlm}. In such detector-free architectures, a general visual encoder functions as a replacement for the detector, from which grid features are generated for subsequent cross-modal fusion, as depicted in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-b}. Thus far, the majority of these approaches primarily concentrate on image understanding tasks, which are typically formulated as classification problems, with only limited attention devoted to generation tasks. In~\cite{xu2021e2e}, the image is encoded using ResNet~\cite{he2016deep} and the performance ($117.3$ CIDEr on COCO~\cite{xu2021e2e}) remains substantially below the state-of-the-art detector-based approach ($129.3$ CIDEr with VinVL-base~\cite{zhang2021multi}). The challenge remains unexplored and inadequately examined concerning 	extit{how to construct a more robust detector-free image captioning model.} Prior endeavors~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,zhang2021multi,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} have established that object tags serve a crucial function in enhancing captioning performance. Rather than extracting object tags from the detector, we introduce a novel fully 	extbf{VI}sion 	extbf{T}ransformer based image 	extbf{CAP}tioning model, termed ViTCAP, with a lightweight Concept Token Network (CTN) that generates concept tokens (see Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-c}). ViTCAP is constructed upon a vision transformer~\cite{dosovitskiy2020image} serving as the primary image encoder. Our vision transformer backbone initiates by encoding the image and generating grid features, upon which the CTN branch is subsequently applied to predict semantic concepts of images. We represent the semantic concepts at the token level rather than the tag level to circumvent tokenization. The multi-modal module subsequently accepts input from both grid representations and Top-$K$ concept tokens for decoding. During training, the CTN is optimized to predict pseudo ground-truth concepts extracted from image captions through a straightforward classification task. We also investigate adopting object tags from the detector as pseudo ground-truth, and empirically observe no additional improvement. Overall, this straightforward design enables the incorporation of semantic concepts into the multi-modal fusion module with rich semantics, and proves critical for enhanced captioning performance. Our ablative analysis demonstrates that, without additional complexities, simple vanilla transformer architecture based {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; 1) substantially outperforms existing detector-free captioning models; 2) exceeds most detector-based models and 3) approaches state-of-the-art detector-based models. Specifically, {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves $138.1$ CIDEr scores on COCO-caption Karpathy split~\cite{lin2014microsoft}, $108.6$ on Google-CC~\cite{sharma2018conceptual}, and $95.4$ on nocaps~\cite{agrawal2019nocaps} datasets. 
oindent To summarize our contributions:  egin{itemize} [leftmargin=8pt] \vspace{-2mm} \item We present a detector-free image captioning model {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\;\!\! with fully transformer architecture, which utilizes grid representations without regional operations. \vspace{-2mm} \item We propose incorporating semantic concepts into end-to-end captioning through learning from open-form captions. We demonstrate that our proposed concept classification training and concept tokens substantially benefit the captioning task. \vspace{-2mm} \item Comprehensive evaluations across multiple captioning datasets validate the effectiveness of our method. {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves competitive or superior results compared to detector-based prior works with distinct inference-time advantages. \end{itemize}

