Image captioning seeks to produce human-readable descriptive text from visual input. This field has experienced remarkable progress, manifested primarily through sophisticated cross-modal fusion architectures~\cite{you2016image,vinyals2015show, xu2015show,rennie2017self,yang2019auto,cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet}; enhanced object-centric features~\cite{anderson2018bottom,zhang2021multi} \& tags~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} derived from pre-trained object detection models; and the acquisition of general 	extbf{	extit{V}}ision and 	extbf{	extit{L}}anguage (VL) representations through large-scale image-text corpora~\cite{zhou2020unified,xu2021e2e,li2020oscar,wang2021simvlm,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing}. However, contemporary mainstream captioning models~\cite{cornia2020meshed,pan2020x,ting2019hierarchy,zhang2021rstnet} exhibit substantial dependence on cumbersome object detectors for regional visual representations in multimodal interaction, as illustrated in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-a}. While object features deliver superior performance, they introduce considerable complications: 1) 	extbf{imposing substantial computational burden} through regional operations (\ie, RPN, RoI Pooling, and NMS). Such intermediate processes inevitably create training inefficiencies and elevated inference latency during prediction~\cite{kim2021vilt,wang2020minivlm}; 2) 	extbf{necessitating box annotations} which severely constrains training and application flexibility. Addressing these limitations, recent research trends favor eliminating detectors from VL pre-training through end-to-end approaches~\cite{jiang2020defense,huang2020pixel,kim2021vilt,yan2021grid,wang2021simvlm}. Such detector-free architectures employ general visual encoders as detector substitutes, generating grid features for subsequent cross-modal fusion, as depicted in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-b}. Currently, most such approaches concentrate on image understanding tasks, typically formulated as classification problems, with limited attention to generation tasks. In~\cite{xu2021e2e}, ResNet~\cite{he2016deep} encodes images, yet performance ($117.3$ CIDEr on COCO~\cite{xu2021e2e}) remains substantially below state-of-the-art detector-based methods ($129.3$ CIDEr with VinVL-base~\cite{zhang2021multi}). The fundamental challenge of 	extit{constructing more robust detector-free image captioning models} remains unexplored and inadequately addressed. Prior research~\cite{li2020oscar,hu2020vivo,zhang2021multi,wang2020minivlm,fang2021compressing} establishes that object tags substantially enhance captioning performance. Rather than extracting object tags from detectors, we present a novel fully 	extbf{VI}sion 	extbf{T}ransformer based image 	extbf{CAP}tioning model, termed ViTCAP, incorporating a lightweight Concept Token Network (CTN) that generates concept tokens (illustrated in Figure~ef{fig:abstract}{\color{darkred}-c}). ViTCAP employs a vision transformer~\cite{dosovitskiy2020image} as its foundational image encoder. Our vision transformer backbone initially encodes images to produce grid features, upon which the CTN branch predicts semantic image concepts. We represent semantic concepts at the token level rather than tag level to circumvent tokenization requirements. The multi-modal module subsequently processes both grid representations and Top-$K$ concept tokens for decoding. During training, CTN optimization targets pseudo ground-truth concepts extracted from image captions through straightforward classification. We additionally explore utilizing detector-derived object tags as pseudo ground-truth, observing no additional improvement empirically. This streamlined design enables semantic concept injection into the multi-modal fusion module with rich semantics, proving essential for enhanced captioning performance. Ablative analysis demonstrates that, without additional complexity, our simple vanilla transformer-based {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; 1) substantially exceeds existing detector-free captioning models; 2) outperforms most detector-based models and 3) approaches state-of-the-art detector-based performance. Specifically, {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; attains $138.1$ CIDEr scores on COCO-caption Karpathy split~\cite{lin2014microsoft}, $108.6$ on Google-CC~\cite{sharma2018conceptual}, and $95.4$ on nocaps~\cite{agrawal2019nocaps} datasets. 
oindent Our contributions are summarized as follows:  egin{itemize} [leftmargin=8pt] \vspace{-2mm} \item We introduce a detector-free image captioning model {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\;\!\! featuring fully transformer architecture, utilizing grid representations without regional operations. \vspace{-2mm} \item We propose semantic concept injection into end-to-end captioning through learning from open-form captions. Our concept classification training and concept tokens demonstrate significant captioning task benefits. \vspace{-2mm} \item Comprehensive evaluations across multiple captioning datasets validate our approach. {
ormalsize ViT}{
ormalsize CAP}\; achieves competitive or superior results compared to detector-based prior work while providing clear inference-time advantages. \end{itemize}

