oindent { f Motivation and Problem:} Mission-critical applications have witnessed widespread deployment of machine learning (ML) systems. The effectiveness of these systems depends critically on both the quantity and quality of training data, alongside the fundamental assumption that deployment-time data distributions remain consistent with and are adequately represented by training samples. This assumption, however, frequently fails to hold. \citet{saria2019tutorial} identify multiple failure modes for ML systems, encompassing data shifts (discrepancies between training and deployment distributions), elevated data variability (including overlapping class labels at decision boundaries) and architectural constraints (such as log-linear boundaries versus neural approaches). Developing trustworthy ML requires robust models and diagnostic tools capable of identifying such failure risks while analyzing their underlying uncertainty sources. Regrettably, these systems commonly exhibit silent failures without providing alerts, even while demonstrating overconfident predictions \cite{nguyen2015deep, jiang2018trust, goodfellow2014explaining}. We tackle the fundamental challenge of predicting, analyzing, and mitigating classifier failure risks. Our objective involves equipping systems with \emph{uncertainty scores} for their predictions, enabling (a) reliable identification of test inputs likely to cause system failures, and (b) recognition of the \emph{uncertainty type} driving the risk, thereby facilitating (c) targeted \emph{mitigation strategies}. Armed with diverse {uncertainty scores}, deployed systems can enhance their robustness when encountering challenging new data points. Consider a scenario where an introspection module signals that the ML system's output carries substantial error probability—the system could abstain and delegate decisions to human experts (avoiding potentially life-threatening consequences). When production data frequently falls outside the training distribution, gathering supplementary training samples and retraining represents a viable solution. The key challenge involves determining appropriate actions under specific conditions. We address this fundamental problem: quantifying uncertainty magnitude and type in deployment inputs, thereby informing decisions about whether and which mitigation approaches are necessary. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent { f State of the Art and its Limitations:} Current approaches for assessing ML prediction trustworthiness rely on confidence scores derived from predictive probabilities, including maximum class probability (MCP) for neural networks \cite{hendrycks2016baseline} or decision boundary distances for SVMs. Nevertheless, predictive probabilities constitute unreliable uncertainty estimates \cite{gal2016dropout, jiang2018trust,nguyen2015deep, goodfellow2014explaining}. Figure ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) illustrates cases where a CNN model incorrectly classifies handwritten digits (MNIST benchmark) while exhibiting high self-confidence scores. Even with calibrated confidence scores (e.g., \cite{platt1999probabilistic,guo2017calibration}), trustworthiness remains questionable since confidence score ordering can be fundamentally unreliable. Calibration methods focus on score scaling, performing monotonic transformations relative to prediction scores without altering confident versus uncertain example rankings. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) demonstrates cases where CNN models assign higher scores to misclassified samples compared to correctly classified ones. Crucially, {confidence scores} fail to capture what models {\em lack knowledge about}. In Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d), Fashion MNIST contains numerous positive training examples resembling shirts like (c) while providing minimal examples similar to (d)—illustrating insufficient training distribution coverage of test-time data. Despite this, the CNN model produces highly confident predictions (0.94 confidence in Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}d). Even state-of-the-art approaches like {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which serves as our primary baseline, exhibit this limitation. Furthermore, and most significantly, {\em confidence scores} remain ``one-dimensional'' without revealing which uncertainty type constitutes the core issue. Consequently, existing confidence scores provide limited guidance for identifying appropriate mitigation strategies. Bayesian methods represent a prominent uncertainty estimation approach \cite{denker1990transforming, barber1998ensemble}, alongside specialized learning algorithm modifications (e.g.,\cite{gal2016dropout, depeweg2018decomposition,lakshminarayanan2017simple,shaker2020aleatoric,ustimenko2020uncertainty}). These approaches, however, require tight integration with specific classification models and necessitate specialized ML pipeline modifications. Consequently, such methods prove inadequate for handling diverse black-box ML systems. \vspace*{0.1cm} 
oindent{ f Proposed Approach:} We introduce \emph{Risk Advisor}, a flexible and comprehensive framework for robustly estimating failure risks across any {pre-trained} black-box classification model. The {\em Risk Advisor} incorporates a post-hoc {\em meta-learner} for uncertainty estimation, operating independently from the base ML system while enabling integration without modifying existing ML pipelines. This {\em meta-learner} maintains model-agnostic properties: it applies to diverse black-box classifier families (including deep neural networks, decision trees, etc). Fig. ef{fig:blockdiagram} presents a schematic representation of our framework. Beyond delivering a {\em risk score} with superior reliability compared to existing methods, the {\em Risk Advisor} offers detailed analysis of underlying uncertainty types driving these risks. We leverage information-theoretic concepts of {\em model uncertainty}, {\em aleatoric uncertainty} and {\em epistemic uncertainty} \cite{hora1996aleatory, der2009aleatory,senge2014reliable}. While these concepts have historical foundations, they have not been previously applied to risk analysis for black-box ML systems, to our knowledge. Our {\em Risk Advisor} quantifies all three risk categories, thereby facilitating informed risk mitigation recommendations based on the specific uncertainty type: \squishlist \item {\em Aleatoric uncertainty} captures data point variability and associated noise surrounding classifier decision boundaries. Elevated values suggest inherent class distinction difficulties, making {\em abstention} rather than forced label assignment the appropriate mitigation strategy. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(a,b) exemplifies high aleatoric uncertainty. \item {\em Epistemic uncertainty} identifies systematic training sample gaps, particularly regions with sparse training coverage but substantial post-deployment test populations. This scenario requires obtaining {\em additional training data} for underrepresented critical regions. Fig. ef{fig:mnist-examples}(c,d) demonstrates high epistemic uncertainty. \item {\em Model uncertainty} signals that black-box ML systems employ models with inadequate learning capacity. The appropriate response involves rebuilding ML systems with enhanced model capacity or more expressive learning architectures, such as deep neural networks replacing log-linear models or Transformers substituting LSTMs or CNNs. \end{list} Our proposed {\em meta-learner} for estimating diverse uncertainty types within the {\em Risk Advisor} framework utilizes an ensemble of $M$ stochastic gradient-boosted decision trees (E-SGBT). Each stochastic gradient boosted tree (SGBT) processes input-output pairs from training samples alongside indicator variables denoting whether the trained black-box ML system incorrectly classified training points. The {\em Risk Advisor's} uncertainty analysis leverages the ensemble's capacity to quantify aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty. Uncertainty scores are computed for training data and deployment-time test data independently to detect gradually emerging risks. \smallskip 
oindent 	extbf{Contributions:} Our primary comparison target is {\em Trust Score} \cite{jiang2018trust}, which similarly operates through model-agnostic post-hoc analysis. Trust Score relies on distances between test points and their training data nearest neighbors. It produces single-valued outputs without guidance for risk type identification. \smallskip This work presents the following novel contributions: \squishlist \item We present the \emph{Risk Advisor} framework, the first \emph{model-agnostic} approach for detecting and mitigating deployment-time failure risks, requiring access {exclusively} to base classifier training data and predictions while accommodating arbitrary Black-box ML models.

